WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:10.120
So welcome to week
three of AA203.

00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:15.880
So as per our usual road
map, today, we are-- today

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:19.320
and tomorrow will be still
in the open-loop quadrant

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:21.360
of our road map.

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:25.320
Specifically last week, so we
introduced the general concept

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:29.960
of open-loop control,
which is to be compared

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:32.159
to with closed-loop
control, which

00:00:32.159 --> 00:00:34.595
will be the topic of next week.

00:00:34.595 --> 00:00:36.220
And specifically, in
open-loop control,

00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:38.880
we highlighted the
fact that we are

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:42.960
interested in control,
an optimal control

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:45.680
signal or trajectory,
which is only

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:47.700
a signal of the initial time t.

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:51.160
So we are looking for an optimal
control trajectory, u of t.

00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:55.340
And specifically, within
this class of methods,

00:00:55.340 --> 00:00:59.980
we discussed that there are
two macro subcategories,

00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:02.100
indirect methods
and direct methods.

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:04.080
Last week, we introduced
indirect methods

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:07.820
which, loosely speaking,
relate with, first of all,

00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:11.080
deriving optimality conditions
for the infinite dimensional

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:14.280
optimal control problem
and solving for those.

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:17.040
If you wish, the analog
we usually recalled

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:19.780
was the one from finite
dimensional optimization,

00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:22.080
whereby the
optimality conditions

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:26.080
were akin to the gradient
of f equal to 0 conditions.

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:28.520
Of course, in finite
dimensional case,

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:31.300
those kind of
optimality conditions,

00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:33.920
so gradient equal
to 0, boil down

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:36.760
to a system of
algebraic equations.

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:39.120
In the case of infinite
dimensional optimization, which

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:41.840
is the one you're interested
in for optimal control--

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:46.240
these optimality condition boils
down to differential equations,

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:49.680
but the high level
picture is the same.

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:51.960
And so today, we will be
finishing our discussion

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:52.980
on indirect methods.

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:58.480
And then on Wednesday, we'll
be looking at direct methods.

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:00.320
And specifically
for today, we will

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:03.560
start by extending the
optimality conditions which

00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:06.080
we introduced last
time, which assumed

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:08.280
that we had unbounded controls.

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:10.000
And so today, we
will be extending

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:11.680
those optimality
conditions specifically

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:14.120
for the case of
bounded controls, which

00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:17.040
is a very common constraint
to have in practice

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:19.740
when you're dealing with
optimal control problems.

00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:22.440
And we will see how this will
lead towards the celebrated

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:26.080
Pontryagin's minimum principle.

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:29.105
We'll then look at various
different examples.

00:02:29.105 --> 00:02:30.480
Specifically,
we'll be looking at

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:34.020
some archetype optimal control
problems, first of all,

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:37.040
to see how we can use
these optimality conditions

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:40.520
in practice and get
familiarity with that but also

00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:43.800
from the perspective of
inspecting the kind of control

00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:48.040
profile that we get out of
these optimality conditions.

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:49.840
And then we'll
conclude this part

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:51.660
by looking at
computational methods.

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:54.680
So we have been describing
indirect methods over and over

00:02:54.680 --> 00:02:59.130
as optimized then discretized
methods, again, in comparison

00:02:59.130 --> 00:03:02.050
to the direct approaches
which are discretized then

00:03:02.050 --> 00:03:04.450
optimized methods,
and specifically

00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:06.390
the optimized part
entailed with deriving

00:03:06.390 --> 00:03:08.890
the optimality conditions and
solving for those differential

00:03:08.890 --> 00:03:11.370
equations, and the
discretized part related

00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:13.250
with feeding those
differential equations

00:03:13.250 --> 00:03:15.250
to a numerical
solver, which is also

00:03:15.250 --> 00:03:19.890
what we'll be looking at with
the computational methods.

00:03:19.890 --> 00:03:22.770
So just as a
reminder, so last time

00:03:22.770 --> 00:03:26.450
we concluded by looking
at this optimal control

00:03:26.450 --> 00:03:30.730
problem where we had assumed
that we had unbounded controls.

00:03:30.730 --> 00:03:33.510
So, again, to recap
the notation here,

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:36.490
we assume that we have a system
described by the following

00:03:36.490 --> 00:03:37.850
dynamics.

00:03:37.850 --> 00:03:41.370
And the goal is to find an
admissible control u star, which

00:03:41.370 --> 00:03:45.170
causes the system to follow
an admissible trajectory, x

00:03:45.170 --> 00:03:48.110
star, that minimizes the
given cost functional,

00:03:48.110 --> 00:03:51.410
which is a combination
of a terminal cost h

00:03:51.410 --> 00:03:56.450
and a state cost g that
is integrated over time.

00:03:56.450 --> 00:04:01.130
And indeed, we assumed that
both the states and the control

00:04:01.130 --> 00:04:06.250
regions were unbounded.

00:04:06.250 --> 00:04:09.930
And this led into defining the
following optimal necessary

00:04:09.930 --> 00:04:13.190
optimality conditions, which
at a high level, entailed,

00:04:13.190 --> 00:04:16.410
first of all, defining the
Hamiltonian, which is you want

00:04:16.410 --> 00:04:20.089
acted as the analog of
the Lagrangian function

00:04:20.089 --> 00:04:22.170
in finite dimensional
case, where

00:04:22.170 --> 00:04:24.770
we basically-- where the
Hamiltonian is defined

00:04:24.770 --> 00:04:31.130
as the state cost plus a term
which represents our dynamic f

00:04:31.130 --> 00:04:34.970
times a vector of cost--
or a function of costates p

00:04:34.970 --> 00:04:39.650
of t, which again represents
the analog of the Lagrangian

00:04:39.650 --> 00:04:42.050
multipliers.

00:04:42.050 --> 00:04:45.230
And infinite dimensional
optimization--

00:04:45.230 --> 00:04:49.570
the Lagrange multipliers
were denoted as lambda.

00:04:49.570 --> 00:04:52.330
Here, we denote these
costates as p, which

00:04:52.330 --> 00:04:54.010
are indeed a function of time.

00:04:54.010 --> 00:04:55.890
And they're called
costates because there

00:04:55.890 --> 00:04:59.770
is one costate variable or
function for every state

00:04:59.770 --> 00:05:01.570
function.

00:05:01.570 --> 00:05:03.870
And so given that we
define this Hamiltonian,

00:05:03.870 --> 00:05:06.050
we saw how the optimality
conditions boil down

00:05:06.050 --> 00:05:08.410
to defining the
set of differential

00:05:08.410 --> 00:05:11.850
equations, the first one
representing trivially

00:05:11.850 --> 00:05:12.790
our dynamics.

00:05:12.790 --> 00:05:15.450
So if we take the partial
derivative of our Hamiltonian

00:05:15.450 --> 00:05:19.150
with respect to the costate
variable p, we get our dynamics,

00:05:19.150 --> 00:05:21.370
so x dot equals f.

00:05:21.370 --> 00:05:25.590
The second two set of
equations are less intuitive,

00:05:25.590 --> 00:05:29.770
but we saw how the second one
essentially boiled down to p

00:05:29.770 --> 00:05:31.250
dot equals the--

00:05:31.250 --> 00:05:33.610
minus the partial derivative
of the Hamiltonian

00:05:33.610 --> 00:05:34.970
with respect to x.

00:05:34.970 --> 00:05:39.730
And then we saw how the third
set of algebraic equations

00:05:39.730 --> 00:05:43.410
represented a stationary
condition of the control

00:05:43.410 --> 00:05:47.070
with respect to the Hamiltonian.

00:05:47.070 --> 00:05:49.690
And so essentially,
that the derivative

00:05:49.690 --> 00:05:53.770
of the Hamiltonian with respect
to u needs to be equal to 0.

00:05:53.770 --> 00:05:56.930
And we saw how in practice this
boiled down very mechanically

00:05:56.930 --> 00:06:00.170
towards usually using this
last set of algebraic equations

00:06:00.170 --> 00:06:03.290
to express u as a
function of x and p.

00:06:03.290 --> 00:06:05.370
We can then do the
appropriate substitutions

00:06:05.370 --> 00:06:09.210
in the differential equations,
which we can then solve.

00:06:09.210 --> 00:06:10.830
To solve these
differential equations,

00:06:10.830 --> 00:06:13.370
we saw how this generated
a-- it boiled down

00:06:13.370 --> 00:06:17.090
into having to pinpoint a number
of constants of integration.

00:06:17.090 --> 00:06:19.810
And we saw how we could use
boundary conditions to pinpoint

00:06:19.810 --> 00:06:22.690
those constants of integration.

00:06:22.690 --> 00:06:26.710
Usually, the initial boundary
conditions are given.

00:06:26.710 --> 00:06:32.650
So we know the state at time 0,
and we know what the time 0 is.

00:06:32.650 --> 00:06:36.130
But the boundary conditions
at the final time

00:06:36.130 --> 00:06:38.570
depend a bit on the scenario
that we're looking at.

00:06:38.570 --> 00:06:42.790
And so we saw how we could use
this master boundary condition,

00:06:42.790 --> 00:06:45.530
depending on whether the
final time or final state

00:06:45.530 --> 00:06:47.050
is free or fixed.

00:06:47.050 --> 00:06:52.210
For example, if the
final state is fixed,

00:06:52.210 --> 00:06:54.550
it means that there are
no admissible variations.

00:06:54.550 --> 00:06:55.970
Delta xf we can do.

00:06:55.970 --> 00:06:59.170
And so all of this
term has to go to 0.

00:06:59.170 --> 00:07:01.610
And so we're left with this
other part of the boundary

00:07:01.610 --> 00:07:05.450
condition and vice versa.

00:07:05.450 --> 00:07:09.990
Any questions on-- until this as
a general recap of where we are?

00:07:17.830 --> 00:07:20.310
And indeed-- so as I
mentioned before, so far,

00:07:20.310 --> 00:07:23.170
we have assumed that both
the admissible controls

00:07:23.170 --> 00:07:27.290
and state are not constrained
by any boundaries.

00:07:27.290 --> 00:07:30.570
But in reality, states
and control constraints

00:07:30.570 --> 00:07:33.370
are actually extremely
common in practice.

00:07:33.370 --> 00:07:36.410
So, for example, a classical
control constraints

00:07:36.410 --> 00:07:40.050
might model that we have
some limits in our actuation.

00:07:40.050 --> 00:07:45.690
So again, we cannot impose a
thrust that is infinitely large.

00:07:45.690 --> 00:07:49.430
And also, state constraints
might be quite common.

00:07:49.430 --> 00:07:52.210
For example, just
to give you an idea,

00:07:52.210 --> 00:07:53.770
a classical state
constraint might

00:07:53.770 --> 00:07:55.770
represent obstacle
avoidance, so where

00:07:55.770 --> 00:08:00.050
we want to avoid some obstacle
in the physical space.

00:08:00.050 --> 00:08:01.710
Now, for the purpose
of this class,

00:08:01.710 --> 00:08:07.290
we'll focus on including the
case of bounds on the controls.

00:08:07.290 --> 00:08:09.370
And we will not
discuss the case where

00:08:09.370 --> 00:08:12.970
we include the bounds or
constraints on the states, first

00:08:12.970 --> 00:08:16.850
of all, because the mechanics
become substantially

00:08:16.850 --> 00:08:20.690
more and more complex and
also because, in practice,

00:08:20.690 --> 00:08:22.890
indirect methods are not
really the tool of choice

00:08:22.890 --> 00:08:25.610
to handle constraints on states.

00:08:25.610 --> 00:08:27.810
So what people
usually do with maybe

00:08:27.810 --> 00:08:30.750
cast the problem as a
motion planning problem--

00:08:30.750 --> 00:08:33.850
we're abstracting away from
the dynamics, or at least

00:08:33.850 --> 00:08:36.450
simplifying the dynamics,
and more geometrically

00:08:36.450 --> 00:08:38.830
solving the trajectory
optimization problem.

00:08:38.830 --> 00:08:40.730
And then you're
using those insights

00:08:40.730 --> 00:08:43.929
that you get from these
more abstract solutions

00:08:43.929 --> 00:08:46.010
to inform your indirect methods.

00:08:46.010 --> 00:08:48.260
So long story short, but
what we're saying here

00:08:48.260 --> 00:08:50.840
is that we want to
expand our analysis,

00:08:50.840 --> 00:08:54.860
and we're going to focus on
the bounds on the controls.

00:08:54.860 --> 00:08:56.250
Yes.

00:08:56.250 --> 00:09:00.680
[INAUDIBLE] last lecture where
we were using the conditions

00:09:00.680 --> 00:09:03.180
for optimal control, the one
you have in the previous slide.

00:09:03.180 --> 00:09:08.100
And then we had a t final equals
some expression in terms of a.

00:09:08.100 --> 00:09:12.500
You were saying that your--

00:09:12.500 --> 00:09:14.900
was that actually-- when
we solved that problem,

00:09:14.900 --> 00:09:18.540
did we actually find that
optimal control for-- this

00:09:18.540 --> 00:09:20.880
is what you're saying that
you could be anything.

00:09:20.880 --> 00:09:22.803
Therefore, tf could be anything.

00:09:22.803 --> 00:09:24.220
And now we're going
to introduce--

00:09:24.220 --> 00:09:27.180
What setting are
you referring to?

00:09:27.180 --> 00:09:31.220
In previous lecture, we did
an example of the t final

00:09:31.220 --> 00:09:32.400
in terms of alpha--

00:09:32.400 --> 00:09:33.940
Yes.

00:09:33.940 --> 00:09:38.260
Did that correspond to some
particular optimal control?

00:09:38.260 --> 00:09:41.600
Or is that what you're saying,
that it was unconstrained

00:09:41.600 --> 00:09:43.600
so the control
could be anything,

00:09:43.600 --> 00:09:45.720
and therefore, the t
final could be anything?

00:09:45.720 --> 00:09:46.220
No.

00:09:46.220 --> 00:09:48.678
So in that case, we actually
solved the problem completely.

00:09:48.678 --> 00:09:51.840
So in this slide, I only
highlighted the final time tf,

00:09:51.840 --> 00:09:53.840
which was a complex thing.

00:09:53.840 --> 00:09:57.420
It's over 1,800 or
something like that.

00:09:57.420 --> 00:10:00.487
But, of course, once we solve
for the necessary optimality

00:10:00.487 --> 00:10:02.820
conditions, so once we solve
the differential equations,

00:10:02.820 --> 00:10:04.000
we have everything.

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:05.080
So we have x.

00:10:05.080 --> 00:10:05.900
We have p.

00:10:05.900 --> 00:10:08.620
And usually, the controls
are a function of x and p.

00:10:08.620 --> 00:10:11.140
And so by just substituting
those x and p that we

00:10:11.140 --> 00:10:13.460
get by solving the
differential equations,

00:10:13.460 --> 00:10:18.020
we would get the
shape of our control.

00:10:18.020 --> 00:10:20.260
So here, what I mean
by bounds on controls

00:10:20.260 --> 00:10:26.500
is that we would have something
like the absolute value of u

00:10:26.500 --> 00:10:29.640
needs to be smaller or equal
than 1, something like that.

00:10:32.540 --> 00:10:34.260
Does this clarify?

00:10:34.260 --> 00:10:35.900
Is it possible that
because we didn't

00:10:35.900 --> 00:10:38.340
have the constraint
in the other problem

00:10:38.340 --> 00:10:42.180
that for some given
alpha and b, it

00:10:42.180 --> 00:10:44.180
could result in the
optimal control that

00:10:44.180 --> 00:10:46.540
violates a constraint?

00:10:46.540 --> 00:10:47.172
Yes, of course.

00:10:47.172 --> 00:10:48.880
And we were not imposing
this constraint.

00:10:48.880 --> 00:10:52.420
So we are not really
sure about if we're

00:10:52.420 --> 00:10:54.560
respecting any kind of
constraint like this.

00:10:54.560 --> 00:10:55.500
Yeah.

00:10:55.500 --> 00:10:57.580
But it will be clear
in a couple of slides

00:10:57.580 --> 00:11:02.460
because we'll be making
some concrete examples.

00:11:02.460 --> 00:11:08.140
So now, before showing you how
the optimality conditions will

00:11:08.140 --> 00:11:10.980
change, I just want to
give you a small insight

00:11:10.980 --> 00:11:14.860
into why the bounds
on the controls

00:11:14.860 --> 00:11:17.220
complicate the analysis.

00:11:17.220 --> 00:11:21.720
And the way we'll build this
intuition is by, as usual,

00:11:21.720 --> 00:11:25.180
referring to the finite
dimensional case.

00:11:25.180 --> 00:11:31.500
And so recall from
our discussions

00:11:31.500 --> 00:11:33.620
on finite dimensional
optimization

00:11:33.620 --> 00:11:36.140
where in order to derive
the optimality conditions

00:11:36.140 --> 00:11:39.180
of the gradient of f
needs to be equal to 0,

00:11:39.180 --> 00:11:43.500
we started by looking
at the increment

00:11:43.500 --> 00:11:48.460
of our function with respect
to some displacement delta x.

00:11:48.460 --> 00:11:50.820
And we saw how this could
be well approximated

00:11:50.820 --> 00:11:58.440
by our function f of x plus the
gradient of f transpose delta x,

00:11:58.440 --> 00:12:07.200
or we call this the differential
plus some higher order terms.

00:12:11.866 --> 00:12:15.100
And essentially, by
just bringing the f

00:12:15.100 --> 00:12:20.380
of x on the other side
of the equation, what

00:12:20.380 --> 00:12:25.500
we get is that the
increment of our function

00:12:25.500 --> 00:12:30.960
is well approximated for a small
delta x's by our differential,

00:12:30.960 --> 00:12:34.180
so the linear part of our
approximation-- of our Taylor

00:12:34.180 --> 00:12:36.620
expansion.

00:12:36.620 --> 00:12:43.260
And we know that for a point x
to be a candidate local minimum,

00:12:43.260 --> 00:12:45.660
what it means is that for
any displacement delta

00:12:45.660 --> 00:12:47.500
x that I make,
this increment has

00:12:47.500 --> 00:12:49.540
to be greater or equal than 0.

00:12:49.540 --> 00:12:51.780
So wherever I move,
essentially, I'm

00:12:51.780 --> 00:12:53.880
increasing the value
of my function.

00:12:57.580 --> 00:13:00.180
Now, in the
unconstrained case, we

00:13:00.180 --> 00:13:03.940
were able to translate this
expansion so that-- specifically

00:13:03.940 --> 00:13:07.060
that the differential has to
be greater or equal than 0

00:13:07.060 --> 00:13:09.500
to the fact that in order
for this to be greater

00:13:09.500 --> 00:13:14.180
or equal than 0, since I can
take any possible displacement

00:13:14.180 --> 00:13:17.740
delta x both in the positive
and in the negative direction,

00:13:17.740 --> 00:13:21.700
the only way for this to be
both greater than, equal than 0

00:13:21.700 --> 00:13:25.620
or less than or equal than
0 is for the gradient of f

00:13:25.620 --> 00:13:28.380
to be equal to 0.

00:13:28.380 --> 00:13:33.780
So this is essentially what we
said a couple of weeks back.

00:13:33.780 --> 00:13:38.420
Now, visually,
what this means is

00:13:38.420 --> 00:13:47.580
that if we have a function
that looks something like this,

00:13:47.580 --> 00:13:49.940
and assuming I'm not
considering the boundary

00:13:49.940 --> 00:13:53.300
points of this function,
the gradient of f equal to 0

00:13:53.300 --> 00:13:57.780
essentially gives us a
mechanism to isolate candidates

00:13:57.780 --> 00:14:01.300
for local minimums, where
specifically, in this case,

00:14:01.300 --> 00:14:04.240
one is a local minimum and
two here are local maximum.

00:14:04.240 --> 00:14:05.940
But still, they're
candidates according

00:14:05.940 --> 00:14:07.560
to our optimality conditions.

00:14:10.500 --> 00:14:15.060
Now, what happens if we do
consider the boundaries here?

00:14:15.060 --> 00:14:17.640
So is this a local minima?

00:14:21.260 --> 00:14:22.620
I see some nodding.

00:14:22.620 --> 00:14:27.060
Can you say why this
is a local minima?

00:14:27.060 --> 00:14:27.740
Yes.

00:14:27.740 --> 00:14:31.460
Because if you move
anywhere on that curve,

00:14:31.460 --> 00:14:33.560
that's still within
your boundaries.

00:14:33.560 --> 00:14:34.420
And they're always--

00:14:34.420 --> 00:14:35.320
Exactly correct.

00:14:35.320 --> 00:14:38.060
So in this case, remember that
here, we're defining minimums

00:14:38.060 --> 00:14:41.810
as whatever movement
admissible delta x I take,

00:14:41.810 --> 00:14:44.230
I'm increasing the
value of my function.

00:14:44.230 --> 00:14:46.710
In this case, the only
displacements I can take

00:14:46.710 --> 00:14:49.650
are ones on the positive
direction on the right.

00:14:49.650 --> 00:14:51.670
And so for all of these
displacements, the value

00:14:51.670 --> 00:14:53.750
my function is greater.

00:14:53.750 --> 00:14:56.870
So this is indeed
a local minimum.

00:14:56.870 --> 00:15:00.310
Same thing for this other
point here, of course,

00:15:00.310 --> 00:15:02.590
mirrored where the only
displacements that I can take

00:15:02.590 --> 00:15:04.510
are in the negative direction.

00:15:04.510 --> 00:15:08.170
So you see that when I'm
considering these boundaries,

00:15:08.170 --> 00:15:12.110
the condition that the
gradient of f is equal to 0

00:15:12.110 --> 00:15:15.270
doesn't really cut it anymore.

00:15:15.270 --> 00:15:21.350
And so going back to this math,
what we know in the bounded case

00:15:21.350 --> 00:15:23.310
is that this has to hold.

00:15:23.310 --> 00:15:28.790
But we're not able to do the
point that these displacements

00:15:28.790 --> 00:15:31.050
can be both in the positive
or negative direction,

00:15:31.050 --> 00:15:32.990
but they depend on the boundary.

00:15:32.990 --> 00:15:37.030
And so the optimality
condition in this case

00:15:37.030 --> 00:15:40.430
is that the differential has
to be greater or equal than 0.

00:15:40.430 --> 00:15:43.070
Now, this complicates a
series of other discussions

00:15:43.070 --> 00:15:45.250
when we actually want
to make this actionable.

00:15:45.250 --> 00:15:47.830
But this is the
difference that it makes

00:15:47.830 --> 00:15:50.790
in the finite dimensional case.

00:15:50.790 --> 00:15:52.570
In the infinite
dimensional case,

00:15:52.570 --> 00:15:55.590
when we move to optimizing
to calculus of variation,

00:15:55.590 --> 00:15:58.110
the exact same
intuition applies.

00:15:58.110 --> 00:16:02.830
And specifically,
remember that we

00:16:02.830 --> 00:16:06.230
derived the optimality
conditions, again,

00:16:06.230 --> 00:16:10.710
starting from the variation of
our cost functional j, which

00:16:10.710 --> 00:16:14.950
we denoted as delta j.

00:16:14.950 --> 00:16:17.710
And in order for
a candidate, you

00:16:17.710 --> 00:16:21.910
start to be a minimum
or a relative minimum.

00:16:21.910 --> 00:16:25.530
We wanted this increment
is greater than 0,

00:16:25.530 --> 00:16:29.670
so exactly as in the
finite dimensional case.

00:16:29.670 --> 00:16:37.510
Now, what we did last week
is that we approximated

00:16:37.510 --> 00:16:41.230
this increment through its
linear term or the variation

00:16:41.230 --> 00:16:44.590
delta j plus some
higher order terms.

00:16:44.590 --> 00:16:46.710
And then we use the
fact that these delta

00:16:46.710 --> 00:16:50.390
u's could be arbitrary to
derive the fundamental theorem

00:16:50.390 --> 00:16:52.230
of calculus of variation.

00:16:52.230 --> 00:16:54.750
That is the delta j, so
the variation delta j

00:16:54.750 --> 00:16:58.250
needed to be equal to 0.

00:16:58.250 --> 00:17:02.670
So I trust that you're
seeing the analog here.

00:17:02.670 --> 00:17:04.510
Now, when we talk about--

00:17:04.510 --> 00:17:06.290
let's open a small parenthesis.

00:17:06.290 --> 00:17:10.390
When we talk about variations
in calculus of variation, so

00:17:10.390 --> 00:17:12.550
what does delta u look like?

00:17:12.550 --> 00:17:16.589
Because a displacement in delta
x intuitively makes sense.

00:17:16.589 --> 00:17:19.849
But here, we're talking
about functions.

00:17:19.849 --> 00:17:22.630
So delta u itself is a function.

00:17:22.630 --> 00:17:34.870
So if you want visually say that
we have a function over time

00:17:34.870 --> 00:17:41.930
and that we have some
bound in our controls,

00:17:41.930 --> 00:17:45.550
which we denote as u bar,
meaning that our controls cannot

00:17:45.550 --> 00:17:52.550
go above this bound, our control
u of t might look something like

00:17:52.550 --> 00:17:56.830
this, where for some time we
are in the admissible region.

00:17:56.830 --> 00:17:59.910
Then we are still admissible
but at the boundary.

00:17:59.910 --> 00:18:01.570
And then perhaps
we could go down.

00:18:01.570 --> 00:18:06.910
So this could be a potential
control function ut.

00:18:06.910 --> 00:18:09.910
So a displacement
delta ut in this case

00:18:09.910 --> 00:18:11.170
is a function in itself.

00:18:11.170 --> 00:18:15.470
So it could be any variation
that I do to my function ut.

00:18:15.470 --> 00:18:19.150
Now, in this part of the region
where I'm not at the boundary,

00:18:19.150 --> 00:18:24.190
the variations that I can take
could be both delta u but also

00:18:24.190 --> 00:18:25.250
negative delta u.

00:18:25.250 --> 00:18:30.350
So variations is both
directions are admissible.

00:18:30.350 --> 00:18:35.350
But in the case where my control
lies at the boundary, then here,

00:18:35.350 --> 00:18:40.370
I can only take the variation
in only on one-sided,

00:18:40.370 --> 00:18:43.310
if that makes sense.

00:18:43.310 --> 00:18:47.050
And so what this boils
down to-- and in general,

00:18:47.050 --> 00:18:50.310
whenever I'm trying
to optimize some--

00:18:53.070 --> 00:18:56.130
could be final time
given some constraints,

00:18:56.130 --> 00:18:59.870
it is very common that u--
for some portion of my control

00:18:59.870 --> 00:19:03.350
profile, I will be at the
boundary at some point.

00:19:03.350 --> 00:19:07.510
And it turns out that if
the optimal control lies

00:19:07.510 --> 00:19:11.910
on a boundary, then the
necessary conditions, similarly

00:19:11.910 --> 00:19:15.030
to how it did in the finite
dimensional case, essentially

00:19:15.030 --> 00:19:19.990
boils down that the
variation delta j has

00:19:19.990 --> 00:19:22.210
to be greater or equal than 0.

00:19:29.070 --> 00:19:36.470
Any questions on how we got to
this update to our conditions?

00:19:36.470 --> 00:19:37.470
Yes.

00:19:37.470 --> 00:19:40.270
Sorry if you mentioned it
before, but on that lower plot

00:19:40.270 --> 00:19:42.790
that you have there,
what was the connection

00:19:42.790 --> 00:19:45.470
to that expression to
the-- you could only

00:19:45.470 --> 00:19:49.530
vary in one direction point?

00:19:49.530 --> 00:19:50.030
Sorry.

00:19:50.030 --> 00:19:51.197
Can you repeat the question?

00:19:51.197 --> 00:19:51.770
So here?

00:19:51.770 --> 00:19:56.710
Yeah, I think I'm confused how
that would be a situation where

00:19:56.710 --> 00:19:57.470
you could only--

00:19:57.470 --> 00:20:00.370
I think you said something
like vary in one direction.

00:20:00.370 --> 00:20:00.870
Yes.

00:20:00.870 --> 00:20:01.912
No, it's a good question.

00:20:01.912 --> 00:20:08.990
So remember that a lot of the
logic that we're following when

00:20:08.990 --> 00:20:10.810
we're doing those
statements over there

00:20:10.810 --> 00:20:15.090
was that the admissible
variations were arbitrary.

00:20:15.090 --> 00:20:17.190
And so this allowed us
to essentially derive

00:20:17.190 --> 00:20:21.190
that the variation of j-- so
delta j has to be equal to 0

00:20:21.190 --> 00:20:23.690
in the fundamental theorem
of calculus of variation.

00:20:23.690 --> 00:20:25.870
So here, I was
trying to visualize

00:20:25.870 --> 00:20:27.430
what does it mean
for, first of all,

00:20:27.430 --> 00:20:30.400
to be a variation in the
space of functions, but then

00:20:30.400 --> 00:20:32.880
also, what does it mean for
a variation to be bounded

00:20:32.880 --> 00:20:35.740
so that it's not arbitrary.

00:20:35.740 --> 00:20:39.260
So in this space of my
control function, here,

00:20:39.260 --> 00:20:41.800
my variations are
actually arbitrary.

00:20:41.800 --> 00:20:43.800
So admissible variations
are arbitrary because I

00:20:43.800 --> 00:20:45.920
could go in both directions.

00:20:45.920 --> 00:20:50.080
Here, when my control
is at a boundary,

00:20:50.080 --> 00:20:54.560
I cannot displace my function in
the positive direction because I

00:20:54.560 --> 00:20:56.720
would violate my bounds.

00:20:56.720 --> 00:20:57.240
Yeah.

00:20:57.240 --> 00:20:58.865
And so the only thing
that I meant here

00:20:58.865 --> 00:21:04.560
is that the boundaries
essentially break the assumption

00:21:04.560 --> 00:21:07.040
that my variations are--

00:21:07.040 --> 00:21:09.120
that any variation
is admissible.

00:21:09.120 --> 00:21:12.320
And so this does not allow
us to make the statement

00:21:12.320 --> 00:21:14.780
that the delta j is equal to 0.

00:21:14.780 --> 00:21:15.280
Yeah.

00:21:22.070 --> 00:21:23.720
Now, this-- of
course, then you can

00:21:23.720 --> 00:21:27.080
imagine that the
consequences of this

00:21:27.080 --> 00:21:31.320
on our optimality conditions
are that we have to strengthen

00:21:31.320 --> 00:21:34.480
our condition on the control u.

00:21:34.480 --> 00:21:38.340
And specifically, these
are the updated conditions,

00:21:38.340 --> 00:21:42.080
which are referred to as the
Pontryagin's minimum principle,

00:21:42.080 --> 00:21:43.320
where we are--

00:21:43.320 --> 00:21:45.820
as it is I highlighted
here on the slide,

00:21:45.820 --> 00:21:48.320
we're essentially
updating and strengthening

00:21:48.320 --> 00:21:52.880
the condition on the
stationarity of our control u.

00:21:52.880 --> 00:21:56.000
So everything stays exactly
the same as the conditions

00:21:56.000 --> 00:22:00.240
that we saw before, with the
addition that-- or modification

00:22:00.240 --> 00:22:01.920
that before.

00:22:01.920 --> 00:22:05.120
The optimal control u had
to be a stationary point

00:22:05.120 --> 00:22:05.980
of the Hamiltonian.

00:22:05.980 --> 00:22:07.800
So the gradient of
h with respect to u

00:22:07.800 --> 00:22:10.120
had to be equal to 0.

00:22:10.120 --> 00:22:12.520
Now we want the
optimal control u

00:22:12.520 --> 00:22:15.680
to be a global minimizer
of the Hamiltonian.

00:22:15.680 --> 00:22:19.120
So essentially, the
Hamiltonian evaluated

00:22:19.120 --> 00:22:22.720
at the optimal
candidate u star is

00:22:22.720 --> 00:22:26.960
a smaller or equal than
the Hamiltonian evaluated

00:22:26.960 --> 00:22:31.460
at any other u of t,
admissible control u.

00:22:36.680 --> 00:22:39.440
Of course, there is
a jump from the fact

00:22:39.440 --> 00:22:41.000
that the variation
has to be greater

00:22:41.000 --> 00:22:47.080
or equal than 0 towards the
updated optimality conditions.

00:22:47.080 --> 00:22:51.080
But the bottom line
here is, again, we

00:22:51.080 --> 00:22:54.000
want to be pragmatic
and functional.

00:22:54.000 --> 00:22:59.160
And when we introduce
bounds on our controls,

00:22:59.160 --> 00:23:02.240
it boils down towards
updating and strengthening

00:23:02.240 --> 00:23:05.120
some of our optimality
conditions, which in this case

00:23:05.120 --> 00:23:09.240
entails having to minimize the
Hamiltonian rather than only

00:23:09.240 --> 00:23:10.820
being a stationary point.

00:23:14.480 --> 00:23:15.860
Any questions here?

00:23:15.860 --> 00:23:18.240
Again, mechanically, we
provided some insight

00:23:18.240 --> 00:23:21.480
into what is more
complex the moment

00:23:21.480 --> 00:23:24.480
we consider bounds on controls.

00:23:24.480 --> 00:23:27.660
And then we stated the
updated optimality conditions.

00:23:36.160 --> 00:23:42.960
So let's now make an example
to operationalize these updated

00:23:42.960 --> 00:23:44.260
optimality conditions.

00:24:02.120 --> 00:24:09.000
And so specifically here, we are
