we will basically have to
solve an optimization problem

00:30:02.730 --> 00:30:05.890
and derive a solution of
u as a function of x and p

00:30:05.890 --> 00:30:08.190
and then doing the
substitution again.

00:30:08.190 --> 00:30:10.422
So mechanically, it
doesn't change much.

00:30:10.422 --> 00:30:12.130
It's just more complex
because before, it

00:30:12.130 --> 00:30:14.830
was enforcing an
algebraic equation.

00:30:14.830 --> 00:30:17.870
Now, it's solving an
optimization problem.

00:30:17.870 --> 00:30:18.370
Yeah.

00:30:23.730 --> 00:30:31.850
So indeed, we want to approach
this minimum energy problem

00:30:31.850 --> 00:30:36.547
where now we have some
bounds on the controls.

00:30:36.547 --> 00:30:38.630
And specifically, again,
we want to be-- as usual,

00:30:38.630 --> 00:30:41.410
we want to be methodical.

00:30:41.410 --> 00:30:53.290
So we define the Hamiltonian as
1/2 x1 squared plus u squared--

00:30:53.290 --> 00:30:56.930
and here, I'm omitting a
dependency on time just to save

00:30:56.930 --> 00:30:57.450
some ink--

00:31:00.162 --> 00:31:09.770
plus the costate times
our dynamics function, p1,

00:31:09.770 --> 00:31:29.500
p2 transpose x2 minus x2 plus
u, which we can write as 1/2 x1

00:31:29.500 --> 00:31:45.080
squared plus u squared plus
p1 x2 minus p2 x2 plus p2 u.

00:31:50.066 --> 00:31:52.580
So this is just our Hamiltonian.

00:31:52.580 --> 00:31:56.580
Now, we want to enforce our
third condition, that is,

00:31:56.580 --> 00:32:00.160
that u has to be a global
minimizer of the Hamiltonian,

00:32:00.160 --> 00:32:02.640
so the new condition that
we just strengthened.

00:32:05.460 --> 00:32:09.160
So we basically want to
solve this problem here.

00:32:09.160 --> 00:32:14.700
So u star has to be a global
minimizer of the Hamiltonian.

00:32:14.700 --> 00:32:17.300
Now, if this is the
Hamiltonian, the only terms

00:32:17.300 --> 00:32:21.300
that depend on u's
are these two here.

00:32:21.300 --> 00:32:29.860
And so this problem essentially
boils down to minimizing over u

00:32:29.860 --> 00:32:34.120
1/2 u squared plus p2 u.

00:32:37.260 --> 00:32:41.100
Is the marker strong
enough this week.

00:32:41.100 --> 00:32:42.860
Not really?

00:32:42.860 --> 00:32:44.700
All right.

00:32:44.700 --> 00:32:47.980
Let's try this other one.

00:32:47.980 --> 00:32:50.400
I love the variance of the--

00:32:50.400 --> 00:32:50.900
yes.

00:32:53.900 --> 00:32:54.520
All right.

00:32:54.520 --> 00:32:58.540
But can you read this one here?

00:32:58.540 --> 00:33:01.880
Anybody that cannot read it?

00:33:01.880 --> 00:33:02.440
All right.

00:33:02.440 --> 00:33:06.760
So notice that this is
a quadratic function.

00:33:06.760 --> 00:33:08.820
So in order to minimize
it, we can just

00:33:08.820 --> 00:33:11.280
set the gradient
of this equal to 0.

00:33:14.060 --> 00:33:18.740
So we can just set the gradient
with respect to u of this thing

00:33:18.740 --> 00:33:26.580
here, so 1/2 u squared
plus p2 u equal to 0,

00:33:26.580 --> 00:33:33.700
which essentially means that u
plus p2 has to be equal to 0.

00:33:33.700 --> 00:33:38.840
And so u star is
equal to minus p2.

00:33:41.803 --> 00:33:43.220
So now to answer
to your question,

00:33:43.220 --> 00:33:45.700
you see here we solved
an optimization problem.

00:33:45.700 --> 00:33:47.700
The output of this
optimization problem

00:33:47.700 --> 00:33:51.360
is still expressing u as a
function of only p in this case.

00:33:51.360 --> 00:33:55.540
But in general, it could
be a function of x and p.

00:33:55.540 --> 00:34:00.880
Now, we have to analyze-- so
we have-- u has some bounds.

00:34:00.880 --> 00:34:03.200
So we have some bounds
on the values of u.

00:34:03.200 --> 00:34:05.220
And so we need to
basically look at

00:34:05.220 --> 00:34:08.780
different case-by-case studies.

00:34:08.780 --> 00:34:20.219
So specifically, we will
be looking at three cases

00:34:20.219 --> 00:34:26.540
where our bounds are that u has
to be between minus 1 and 1.

00:34:26.540 --> 00:34:28.179
And so we'll be
looking at the case

00:34:28.179 --> 00:34:32.739
where this global
minimizer, minus p2,

00:34:32.739 --> 00:34:36.020
is smaller than negative 1.

00:34:36.020 --> 00:34:42.500
We'll be looking at minus p2
being between minus 1 and 1

00:34:42.500 --> 00:34:47.639
or the fact that minus
p2 is greater than 1.

00:34:47.639 --> 00:34:49.420
We'll be looking at
these three cases,

00:34:49.420 --> 00:34:52.620
because ideally, the optimal
control will be negative p2,

00:34:52.620 --> 00:34:57.900
but there are some bounds on
u that we have to respect.

00:34:57.900 --> 00:35:02.940
Visually, if you want to have
a sense of what is happening,

00:35:02.940 --> 00:35:07.540
this is a quadratic function, so
something that looks like this.

00:35:07.540 --> 00:35:11.380
The minimum is at negative p2.

00:35:11.380 --> 00:35:15.100
And now we'll be analyzing
different cases depending

00:35:15.100 --> 00:35:18.180
on where minus p2 falls.

00:35:18.180 --> 00:35:22.800
So the most obvious one is
if minus p2 is feasible--

00:35:22.800 --> 00:35:25.820
so it falls between
negative 1 and 1, so, say,

00:35:25.820 --> 00:35:27.080
something like this--

00:35:31.020 --> 00:35:35.340
then the optimal u
would just be minus p2.

00:35:41.900 --> 00:35:48.100
Now, say that negative p2
is smaller than minus 1.

00:35:48.100 --> 00:35:54.000
So we are in a situation
where this is negative.

00:35:54.000 --> 00:35:56.300
Say that 1, 1 is here.

00:35:56.300 --> 00:35:59.240
What would be the
optimum in this case,

00:35:59.240 --> 00:36:00.520
the optimal u in this case?

00:36:07.140 --> 00:36:09.780
So this is the function
we want to minimize,

00:36:09.780 --> 00:36:12.220
which is our Hamiltonian.

00:36:12.220 --> 00:36:19.220
We can only choose
within minus 1 and 1.

00:36:19.220 --> 00:36:21.520
So in this case, where
would the minimum be?

00:36:21.520 --> 00:36:22.020
Yes.

00:36:22.020 --> 00:36:22.980
It's minus 1.

00:36:22.980 --> 00:36:26.620
Yeah, exactly.

00:36:26.620 --> 00:36:30.400
So in this case, the
minimum would be minus 1.

00:36:30.400 --> 00:36:32.820
And you can imagine
a similar argument

00:36:32.820 --> 00:36:36.280
if negative p2 is
greater than 1.

00:36:36.280 --> 00:36:40.500
So the interval now sits
on the left of negative p2.

00:36:40.500 --> 00:36:43.680
And the minimum here would be 1.

00:36:48.260 --> 00:36:50.620
So essentially,
now we have solved

00:36:50.620 --> 00:36:53.440
the third and
strengthened condition.

00:36:53.440 --> 00:36:57.940
So we found the global
minimum of the Hamiltonian

00:36:57.940 --> 00:36:59.660
with respect to u.

00:36:59.660 --> 00:37:01.180
And now we basically
have what we

00:37:01.180 --> 00:37:03.140
need because now we
have an expression of u

00:37:03.140 --> 00:37:05.450
as a function of x and p.

00:37:05.450 --> 00:37:08.310
In this case, only
the costates p,

00:37:08.310 --> 00:37:12.190
which we can substitute in the
differential equations above,

00:37:12.190 --> 00:37:15.210
feed them into a solver
and get the solution out.

00:37:18.230 --> 00:37:21.610
Interestingly enough--
and this is, I think,

00:37:21.610 --> 00:37:26.750
one of the strong points
of indirect methods

00:37:26.750 --> 00:37:29.130
is that regardless
of the solution,

00:37:29.130 --> 00:37:32.150
so what the exact control
is, we have basically

00:37:32.150 --> 00:37:36.030
pinpointed the structure of
how our control looks like.

00:37:36.030 --> 00:37:46.490
So now, let's plot our
control u as a function of p2.

00:37:50.910 --> 00:37:52.950
Well, we have now is
that our control in

00:37:52.950 --> 00:37:56.550
between minus 1 and 1.

00:37:56.550 --> 00:37:59.590
So if p2 is between
minus 1 and 1,

00:37:59.590 --> 00:38:03.130
it will basically look
something like this.

00:38:07.190 --> 00:38:14.130
On the other hand, when
it is below negative 1,

00:38:14.130 --> 00:38:17.130
it will be saturating at 1.

00:38:17.130 --> 00:38:22.810
And when it is above 1, it
will be saturating at minus 1.

00:38:22.810 --> 00:38:25.830
So our control
profile is essentially

00:38:25.830 --> 00:38:28.610
linear and saturating
after some point.

00:38:34.030 --> 00:38:35.630
Now, of course, in
the moment where

00:38:35.630 --> 00:38:38.430
we didn't have those
bounds, our optimal control

00:38:38.430 --> 00:38:40.630
would just be the linear point.

00:38:45.210 --> 00:38:46.810
Any questions on this?

00:38:50.630 --> 00:38:51.990
Yeah.

00:38:51.990 --> 00:38:56.610
I understand this is the third
condition of the Pontryagin's

00:38:56.610 --> 00:38:58.550
minimum principle.

00:38:58.550 --> 00:39:00.310
What about the other two?

00:39:00.310 --> 00:39:02.210
Yes, it's a good question.

00:39:02.210 --> 00:39:04.230
So basically, now
here, we express u

00:39:04.230 --> 00:39:06.270
as a function of x and p.

00:39:06.270 --> 00:39:11.130
The mechanics of this go that
then we take this expression.

00:39:11.130 --> 00:39:15.670
We substitute it in the other
two differential equation

00:39:15.670 --> 00:39:16.725
conditions.

00:39:16.725 --> 00:39:18.350
And we get some
differential equations,

00:39:18.350 --> 00:39:22.470
which we then solve either
analytically as we did last week

00:39:22.470 --> 00:39:25.870
or as we will see today
through numerical solvers.

00:39:25.870 --> 00:39:27.230
So this is the best one.

00:39:27.230 --> 00:39:28.070
Yes.

00:39:28.070 --> 00:39:30.950
So here, we basically
stopped halfway there.

00:39:30.950 --> 00:39:34.070
So we just derived
the profile of u.

00:39:34.070 --> 00:39:36.230
So it's just the
strengthened condition.

00:39:36.230 --> 00:39:38.350
If you had to go
all the way through,

00:39:38.350 --> 00:39:41.070
you would substitute this in
the other differential equation

00:39:41.070 --> 00:39:43.070
then solve those
differential equations.

00:39:43.070 --> 00:39:45.230
Here, we just stopped here.

00:39:45.230 --> 00:39:48.450
But it's already said that
already at this point,

00:39:48.450 --> 00:39:52.350
we can basically inspect the
structure of our control,

00:39:52.350 --> 00:39:55.110
because whenever we will solve
those differential equations,

00:39:55.110 --> 00:39:57.550
we will get x's and p's.

00:39:57.550 --> 00:40:00.030
And those x's and
p's-- in this case,

00:40:00.030 --> 00:40:04.250
only p will allow us to pinpoint
our actual control, which,

00:40:04.250 --> 00:40:06.950
regardless of the value of p,
will look like something like

00:40:06.950 --> 00:40:08.950
this.

00:40:08.950 --> 00:40:12.670
How do we use the
tf [INAUDIBLE]?

00:40:12.670 --> 00:40:14.830
Those will be used to
pinpoint the-- those

00:40:14.830 --> 00:40:16.390
will be boundary
conditions, which

00:40:16.390 --> 00:40:19.470
we use to pinpoint the
constants of integration

00:40:19.470 --> 00:40:20.970
in our differential equations.

00:40:23.850 --> 00:40:25.350
Yes.

00:40:25.350 --> 00:40:27.450
So for the example graph
that you drew there

00:40:27.450 --> 00:40:30.950
that explained how you found
conditions, what would--

00:40:30.950 --> 00:40:35.310
is the x-axis-- what axis
represents what-- which one is u

00:40:35.310 --> 00:40:37.870
and which one-- or
[INAUDIBLE] misunderstanding.

00:40:37.870 --> 00:40:40.350
Yeah, so this would
be u, and this

00:40:40.350 --> 00:40:43.250
would be the Hamiltonian
as a function of u.

00:40:47.070 --> 00:40:48.810
Does that make sense?

00:40:48.810 --> 00:40:49.310
Yeah.

00:40:54.590 --> 00:40:56.950
Any other questions on this?

00:40:56.950 --> 00:40:59.490
We will see three more examples.

00:40:59.490 --> 00:41:03.370
So now we'll go through
a few of these cases.

00:41:08.870 --> 00:41:11.750
And so indeed-- so
exactly what we get

00:41:11.750 --> 00:41:14.350
is this control,
so essentially what

00:41:14.350 --> 00:41:17.330
we derived here on the
slide-- on the whiteboard.

00:41:21.710 --> 00:41:26.630
As a caveat, Pontryagin
and colleagues

00:41:26.630 --> 00:41:29.030
derived some additional
necessary conditions

00:41:29.030 --> 00:41:32.650
depending on the exact
structure of the problem.

00:41:32.650 --> 00:41:34.110
These are not too
relevant for us

00:41:34.110 --> 00:41:36.190
for the purposes of
this class, but I just

00:41:36.190 --> 00:41:39.910
wanted to mention that there
are some additional optimality

00:41:39.910 --> 00:41:41.750
conditions that were derived.

00:41:41.750 --> 00:41:45.030
Specifically, if the
final time is fixed

00:41:45.030 --> 00:41:51.390
and the Hamiltonian does not
depend explicitly on time,

00:41:51.390 --> 00:41:55.270
then the optimality condition
is that the Hamiltonian-- it

00:41:55.270 --> 00:42:00.160
has to be constant for all
times between t0 and tf.

00:42:00.160 --> 00:42:03.800
And on the other hand, if the
final time and the Hamiltonian

00:42:03.800 --> 00:42:07.080
is free and Hamiltonian does
not depend explicitly on time,

00:42:07.080 --> 00:42:09.440
then the Hamiltonian
has to be equal to 0

00:42:09.440 --> 00:42:13.200
for all times between t0 and tf.

00:42:13.200 --> 00:42:14.992
Now, again, do not read
too much into this.

00:42:14.992 --> 00:42:17.033
This is just-- I wanted
to use this as an example

00:42:17.033 --> 00:42:19.680
to mention that there have been
developed additional optimality

00:42:19.680 --> 00:42:22.320
conditions which, at
the end of the day,

00:42:22.320 --> 00:42:25.840
are useful to pinpoint
even further the structure

00:42:25.840 --> 00:42:27.120
of your solution.

00:42:27.120 --> 00:42:29.880
So there are a number
of optimality conditions

00:42:29.880 --> 00:42:31.920
which have been
developed, and these

00:42:31.920 --> 00:42:34.920
are additional ones compared to
the ones that we showed before.

00:42:34.920 --> 00:42:38.000
And if your problem falls
within these buckets, then

00:42:38.000 --> 00:42:40.520
the more optimality conditions
you have, the better,

00:42:40.520 --> 00:42:42.440
because you can
characterize even

00:42:42.440 --> 00:42:45.140
further what your optimal
solution looks like.

00:42:47.680 --> 00:42:49.680
So what we'll be doing
now is that we'll

00:42:49.680 --> 00:42:54.200
be looking at three archetypes
essentially of optimal control

00:42:54.200 --> 00:42:55.060
problems.

00:42:55.060 --> 00:42:58.580
Specifically, we'll be looking
at minimum time problems,

00:42:58.580 --> 00:43:01.600
so get to your goal
as fast as possible.

00:43:01.600 --> 00:43:05.040
We'll be looking at minimum
fuel problems, where

00:43:05.040 --> 00:43:08.000
the costs will be to
minimize the absolute value

00:43:08.000 --> 00:43:10.480
of the control effort.

00:43:10.480 --> 00:43:14.800
Again, you could see this as
the analog of minimizing thrusts

00:43:14.800 --> 00:43:17.720
if you're looking at
aerospace applications.

00:43:17.720 --> 00:43:22.120
And then we'll be looking
at minimum energy problems.

00:43:22.120 --> 00:43:26.520
And so starting from
minimum time problems,

00:43:26.520 --> 00:43:28.920
we'll be looking at the
case where indeed we have

00:43:28.920 --> 00:43:30.740
some bounds on the controls.

00:43:30.740 --> 00:43:36.080
So the control u has to be
between some lower bound, Mi

00:43:36.080 --> 00:43:39.880
minus and some
upper bound Mi plus.

00:43:39.880 --> 00:43:43.500
And we want to drive a
control affine system.

00:43:43.500 --> 00:43:45.480
So for these examples,
we will always

00:43:45.480 --> 00:43:48.520
be assuming the exact
same system, which

00:43:48.520 --> 00:43:52.560
is a control affine system,
in the sense that it linearly

00:43:52.560 --> 00:43:56.520
depends on the control ut.

00:43:56.520 --> 00:44:00.400
But this may potentially depend
nonlinearly on the state x.

00:44:00.400 --> 00:44:05.320
And this is because the a and
b might be nonlinear in x.

00:44:05.320 --> 00:44:10.920
But this still-- so it is
fairly general, dynamical system

00:44:10.920 --> 00:44:13.800
and formulation, but it does
simplify slightly things

00:44:13.800 --> 00:44:18.200
because it is linear
in the controls.

00:44:18.200 --> 00:44:20.520
And, again, the purpose
of minimum time problems

00:44:20.520 --> 00:44:23.080
is to get to a goal
as fast as possible.

00:44:23.080 --> 00:44:26.460
In this case, we want to start
from an arbitrary state x0,

00:44:26.460 --> 00:44:28.700
and we want to reach the origin.

00:44:28.700 --> 00:44:31.400
And the way we encode
this minimum time--

00:44:31.400 --> 00:44:34.120
the minimization of time
in terms of a functional

00:44:34.120 --> 00:44:36.440
is that we want to
minimize j, which

00:44:36.440 --> 00:44:39.860
is just the integral
between t0 and tf of 1.

00:44:39.860 --> 00:44:42.700
So we're just summing
time essentially.

00:44:42.700 --> 00:44:43.200
Yes.

00:44:43.200 --> 00:44:45.780
What does i represent here?

00:44:45.780 --> 00:44:47.360
Is it a time index?

00:44:47.360 --> 00:44:52.320
Or is it a different
possible admissible controls?

00:44:52.320 --> 00:44:55.740
We are assuming that u here
could be a multivariate.

00:44:55.740 --> 00:44:59.680
And so we have M
control variables.

00:44:59.680 --> 00:45:00.730
Yeah.

00:45:00.730 --> 00:45:01.230
Oh.

00:45:01.230 --> 00:45:03.070
And then would you
know if there's

00:45:03.070 --> 00:45:04.680
a comparison [INAUDIBLE]?

00:45:04.680 --> 00:45:05.740
Yes.

00:45:05.740 --> 00:45:08.080
So imagine maybe
you can accelerate

00:45:08.080 --> 00:45:09.480
in different directions.

00:45:09.480 --> 00:45:11.000
And for each of
those dimensions,

00:45:11.000 --> 00:45:12.580
you have some limits.

00:45:18.480 --> 00:45:28.660
So let's apply what we
know to solve this problem.

00:45:51.844 --> 00:45:56.260
So as usual, we want to start
from defining the Hamiltonian.

00:45:56.260 --> 00:46:01.940
So the Hamiltonian for us
entails the state cost g,

00:46:01.940 --> 00:46:09.660
which for us is just 1 plus the
costates times our dynamics.

00:46:09.660 --> 00:46:17.860
And so in this case, it will
be p transpose a plus b.

00:46:21.280 --> 00:46:25.760
Now, just as a
notation, here, we

00:46:25.760 --> 00:46:33.040
will express b, the matrix b,
as in terms of its columns.

00:46:33.040 --> 00:46:46.640
So we can just rewrite b in this
way, which essentially means

00:46:46.640 --> 00:46:57.840
that this is equal to 1 plus p
transpose a plus the sum that

00:46:57.840 --> 00:47:05.160
goes from i to m of bi, ui.

00:47:05.160 --> 00:47:07.480
And this is because
then this product

00:47:07.480 --> 00:47:11.880
here essentially becomes
the linear combination

00:47:11.880 --> 00:47:13.340
of its columns, essentially.

00:47:18.850 --> 00:47:22.600
And we can rewrite this
as 1 plus p transpose

00:47:22.600 --> 00:47:28.400
a plus the summation
that goes from 1 to m

00:47:28.400 --> 00:47:32.980
of p transpose bi, ui.

00:47:38.060 --> 00:47:41.360
So here, we just
wrote our Hamiltonian.

00:47:41.360 --> 00:47:45.040
Now, as we did before,
we want to start

00:47:45.040 --> 00:47:48.850
from the third-order optimality
conditions, which in this case

00:47:48.850 --> 00:47:52.750
and according to the
Pontryagin's minimum principle,

00:47:52.750 --> 00:47:55.090
it means that we
want to find u, that

00:47:55.090 --> 00:47:58.770
is, the global minimizer
of the Hamiltonian.

00:47:58.770 --> 00:48:04.210
So, as usual, u star
will be the minimum

00:48:04.210 --> 00:48:06.610
with respect to u
of the Hamiltonian,

00:48:06.610 --> 00:48:09.170
which, again, out of
these three terms,

00:48:09.170 --> 00:48:11.410
only the last one depends on u.

00:48:11.410 --> 00:48:20.610
And so this essentially means
that we want to minimize u

00:48:20.610 --> 00:48:24.190
of this summation within
1m of that term over there,

00:48:24.190 --> 00:48:29.090
so p transpose bi, ui.

00:48:29.090 --> 00:48:33.290
Now, the controls are
something that we pick.

00:48:33.290 --> 00:48:37.330
And these are independent
according to our problem

00:48:37.330 --> 00:48:40.770
formulation across m.

00:48:40.770 --> 00:48:44.450
And so minimizing the
summation is essentially

00:48:44.450 --> 00:48:47.570
equivalent to minimizing
the individual submission

00:48:47.570 --> 00:48:50.510
terms for all i's
that go from 1 to m.

00:48:53.490 --> 00:48:56.610
And so this basically
maps to minimizing over

00:48:56.610 --> 00:49:05.710
ui of p transpose bi, ui for
all i's that goes from 1 to m.

00:49:13.210 --> 00:49:18.090
Now, does this make sense?

00:49:18.090 --> 00:49:19.370
Yes.

00:49:19.370 --> 00:49:22.230
Now, how do we minimize this?

00:49:22.230 --> 00:49:25.970
So, again, I think it's good
to consider different cases.

00:49:25.970 --> 00:49:31.050
So specifically, our
optimal control u star

00:49:31.050 --> 00:49:38.050
will depend on whether p
transpose bi is positive

00:49:38.050 --> 00:49:42.250
or whether p transpose
bi is negative.

00:49:42.250 --> 00:49:46.170
So if p transpose
bi is positive,

00:49:46.170 --> 00:49:49.110
what is the minimizer
u in this case?

00:49:57.490 --> 00:50:01.970
So we want to minimize this term
over here as a function of u.

00:50:01.970 --> 00:50:06.410
This will depend on what
this term in front of u is.

00:50:06.410 --> 00:50:12.450
And so if this term is positive
and u has to be between Mi minus

00:50:12.450 --> 00:50:16.770
and Mi plus, how would
you pick u in this case?

00:50:16.770 --> 00:50:18.410
Negative x's.

00:50:18.410 --> 00:50:20.450
Yeah, correct, exactly.

00:50:20.450 --> 00:50:29.530
So Mi And, of course, by
mirroring, in case p transpose

00:50:29.530 --> 00:50:32.330
bi is negative, what is u here?

00:50:32.330 --> 00:50:37.870
It will be the upper
bound, so Mi plus.

00:50:40.530 --> 00:50:45.223
Now, are we missing
something here?

00:50:45.223 --> 00:50:46.210
0.

00:50:46.210 --> 00:50:47.590
Yeah, exactly.

00:50:47.590 --> 00:50:53.250
And the point here is
that for p transpose

00:50:53.250 --> 00:50:58.570
bi, for optimal controls
for which p transpose bi is

00:50:58.570 --> 00:51:02.290
optimal triplets, so optimal
x, u, and p, for which

00:51:02.290 --> 00:51:06.850
p transpose bi's is equal to 0,
the optimality conditions do not

00:51:06.850 --> 00:51:10.330
give us any characterization
of any information about what

00:51:10.330 --> 00:51:13.210
the optimal control looks like.

00:51:13.210 --> 00:51:22.130
This is a condition that is
referred to as singular arcs

00:51:22.130 --> 00:51:25.450
or singular conditions,
which essentially says

00:51:25.450 --> 00:51:32.450
that the optimality conditions
for some characteristics

00:51:32.450 --> 00:51:35.610
of our triplet do not
give us any information

00:51:35.610 --> 00:51:38.570
about the optimal control u.

00:51:38.570 --> 00:51:40.990
Now, for the purposes
of this class,

00:51:40.990 --> 00:51:42.950
we won't be dealing
with singular arcs,

00:51:42.950 --> 00:51:46.690
and so this is not a problem
that should bother you too much.

00:51:46.690 --> 00:51:50.610
But I just wanted to
use this example as--

00:51:50.610 --> 00:51:53.330
indeed an example
of the problems

00:51:53.330 --> 00:51:56.710
that could emerge,
whereby in this case,

00:51:56.710 --> 00:52:00.970
there could be the situation
where my optimal is actually

00:52:00.970 --> 00:52:05.850
lies in a situation where
for some portion of my time,

00:52:05.850 --> 00:52:07.870
p transpose bi is equal to 0.

00:52:07.870 --> 00:52:11.130
And so I would not know how
to characterize my controls.

00:52:11.130 --> 00:52:13.770
Now, there could be some
much more sophisticated math

00:52:13.770 --> 00:52:17.830
in order to pinpoint the
behavior of my control

00:52:17.830 --> 00:52:18.950
in this case.

00:52:18.950 --> 00:52:20.710
It is easier for linear systems.

00:52:20.710 --> 00:52:25.770
It becomes much a
problem-depending solution

00:52:25.770 --> 00:52:28.970
in the moment where you're
looking at nonlinear systems.

00:52:28.970 --> 00:52:30.850
But I think it's just
important for you

00:52:30.850 --> 00:52:32.450
to know that there
might be situations

00:52:32.450 --> 00:52:35.530
where these problems emerge.

00:52:35.530 --> 00:52:38.770
And in a way, this is actually
where most of the brainpower,

00:52:38.770 --> 00:52:41.530
when you try to use
indirect methods goes into,

00:52:41.530 --> 00:52:46.130
is tackling these
ill-conditioned situations.

00:52:46.130 --> 00:52:51.515
For us, assuming
that the optimal--

00:52:51.515 --> 00:52:52.890
for our optimal
control problem--

00:52:52.890 --> 00:52:54.690
so the optimal
triplet x, p, and u

00:52:54.690 --> 00:52:57.970
does not fall into the
singular conditions--

00:52:57.970 --> 00:53:00.610
we basically have
determined that our control

00:53:00.610 --> 00:53:03.250
has this form
here, which is also

00:53:03.250 --> 00:53:06.250
known as a bang-bang control.

00:53:06.250 --> 00:53:08.810
And this is because
essentially, the control

00:53:08.810 --> 00:53:13.890
has a profile that either
accelerates at max velocity

00:53:13.890 --> 00:53:16.330
or breaks at its maximum.

00:53:16.330 --> 00:53:19.330
And if you think about what
we were doing here, that is,

00:53:19.330 --> 00:53:21.930
minimizing final
time, it makes sense.

00:53:21.930 --> 00:53:25.890
So we have some linear system
or a control affine system.

00:53:25.890 --> 00:53:27.110
We want to minimize time.

00:53:27.110 --> 00:53:29.070
And so we're not messing around.

00:53:29.070 --> 00:53:33.770
We either go full throttle, or
we break at our maximum speed.

00:53:33.770 --> 00:53:34.670
Yes.

00:53:34.670 --> 00:53:36.950
So [INAUDIBLE] is equal to 0.

00:53:36.950 --> 00:53:38.690
Does that not necessarily
mean that it's

00:53:38.690 --> 00:53:40.580
uncontrollable at that state?

00:53:40.580 --> 00:53:42.820
It means that the
optimality conditions do not

00:53:42.820 --> 00:53:46.160
give us a characterization of
how the control looks like.

00:53:46.160 --> 00:53:49.980
So remember, all of these
are optimality conditions

00:53:49.980 --> 00:53:53.260
that allow us to pinpoint
candidates for optimality.

00:53:53.260 --> 00:53:55.320
And so this means that
in those situations,

00:53:55.320 --> 00:53:57.820
these optimality conditions
do not give us information

00:53:57.820 --> 00:53:59.540
on what an optimal looks like.

00:53:59.540 --> 00:54:02.340
Yes.

00:54:02.340 --> 00:54:04.340
What is singular arcs?

00:54:04.340 --> 00:54:07.700
What types of problems
these are unique to?

00:54:07.700 --> 00:54:09.480
They're not unique
to specific problems,

00:54:09.480 --> 00:54:11.940
but singular arc or
singular condition

00:54:11.940 --> 00:54:16.780
refers to those situations for
which the optimality conditions

00:54:16.780 --> 00:54:21.020
are not able to characterize
the control essentially.

00:54:21.020 --> 00:54:24.520
It's not new to the
application of the Pontryagin's

00:54:24.520 --> 00:54:25.980
minimum principle?

00:54:25.980 --> 00:54:26.560
Yes, yes.

00:54:26.560 --> 00:54:29.960
So if you're applying the
Pontryagin's minimum principle,

00:54:29.960 --> 00:54:32.780
that is, the optimality
conditions for optimal control

00:54:32.780 --> 00:54:38.300
problems, you might end up in
situations like these where we--

00:54:38.300 --> 00:54:40.740
at face value, we don't
have enough information

00:54:40.740 --> 00:54:42.760
to characterize what
the control looks like.

00:54:48.120 --> 00:54:48.620
Sorry.

00:54:48.620 --> 00:54:51.760
You said this already,
but I'm just confused.

00:54:51.760 --> 00:54:54.740
Did you get that the controller
is a bang-bang controller

00:54:54.740 --> 00:55:00.100
from minimizing the Hamiltonian,
or the problem gave you

00:55:00.100 --> 00:55:01.820
that this is a
bang-bang controller?

00:55:01.820 --> 00:55:06.420
So, again, here, I think the
mental mindset you should be in

00:55:06.420 --> 00:55:09.117
is that we are being
extremely methodical.

00:55:09.117 --> 00:55:10.700
So we have an optimal
control problem,

00:55:10.700 --> 00:55:12.240
and we follow the recipe.

00:55:12.240 --> 00:55:15.220
So the recipe entails
defining the Hamiltonian

00:55:15.220 --> 00:55:18.580
and then enforcing the
optimality conditions.

00:55:18.580 --> 00:55:21.100
The way we mechanically
go through the optimality

00:55:21.100 --> 00:55:24.220
conditions is by starting
from the third one, which

00:55:24.220 --> 00:55:28.540
is the, in this case, finding
use the globally minimize

00:55:28.540 --> 00:55:32.460
the Hamiltonian, which
leads us to this.

00:55:32.460 --> 00:55:36.420
If we were to continue, we would
then take this and substitute it

00:55:36.420 --> 00:55:40.420
in the differential equations.

00:55:40.420 --> 00:55:42.460
We would then solve those
differential equations

00:55:42.460 --> 00:55:45.060
and get the full thing.

00:55:45.060 --> 00:55:48.060
Here, we're, again,
stopping before the solution

00:55:48.060 --> 00:55:51.300
of the differential equations,
that is, we only get

00:55:51.300 --> 00:55:53.960
the global minimum
of the Hamiltonian.

00:55:53.960 --> 00:55:56.060
And this already
gives us the profile

00:55:56.060 --> 00:55:59.220
of our control signal,
which, in this case,

00:55:59.220 --> 00:56:02.700
is bang-bang, because we already
know that we will basically

00:56:02.700 --> 00:56:08.180
be at the maximum control on
the boundaries of our control

00:56:08.180 --> 00:56:12.420
at all times essentially,
removing the singular arc

00:56:12.420 --> 00:56:13.300
discussion.

00:56:13.300 --> 00:56:13.800
Yeah.

00:56:18.380 --> 00:56:24.300
And so essentially,
we showed what

00:56:24.300 --> 00:56:28.420
to do when p transpose
b is different from 0.

00:56:28.420 --> 00:56:32.260
It is not obvious what we
do in the equal to 0 case.

00:56:32.260 --> 00:56:34.020
And we discussed this--

00:56:34.020 --> 00:56:37.260
we refer to these situations are
singular conditions or singular

00:56:37.260 --> 00:56:39.220
arcs.

00:56:39.220 --> 00:56:42.500
And I do-- if you're
interested in learning more,

00:56:42.500 --> 00:56:44.940
the textbook from Kurt we
were referring to last week

00:56:44.940 --> 00:56:46.420
is indeed a good
reference for this

00:56:46.420 --> 00:56:48.320
because it has some discussions.

00:56:51.980 --> 00:56:57.180
OK, so now we move towards a
second archetype optimal control

00:56:57.180 --> 00:57:00.140
problem, which is minimum fuel.

00:57:00.140 --> 00:57:05.420
So in this case, we
have the same boundary--

00:57:05.420 --> 00:57:06.720
bounds on the controls.

00:57:06.720 --> 00:57:09.500
We have the exact same
control affine system.

00:57:09.500 --> 00:57:14.700
But now we are changing
the cost, whereby we

00:57:14.700 --> 00:57:16.480
start from an arbitrary x of 0.

00:57:16.480 --> 00:57:19.200
We want to reach the
origin in a fixed time.

00:57:19.200 --> 00:57:20.260
So now, time is fixed.

00:57:20.260 --> 00:57:22.420
It's not a variable
we're optimizing over.

00:57:22.420 --> 00:57:27.620
And we want to reach the origin
by minimizing the absolute value

00:57:27.620 --> 00:57:29.340
of the control.

00:57:29.340 --> 00:57:30.900
And these types
of problems where

00:57:30.900 --> 00:57:32.780
minimizing absolute
values of the controls

00:57:32.780 --> 00:57:38.820
are usually referred to
as minimum fuel problems.

00:57:38.820 --> 00:57:49.460
So, again, let's follow
the usual procedure.

00:57:49.460 --> 00:57:53.140
So we define the Hamiltonian
which, in this case,

00:57:53.140 --> 00:57:58.980
g for us is the sum
from 1 to m of ci times

00:57:58.980 --> 00:58:06.460
the absolute value of ui
plus p transpose a plus

00:58:06.460 --> 00:58:14.100
the sum that goes from 1
to m of p transpose bi, ui.

00:58:14.100 --> 00:58:16.640
Here, I skipped a
couple of steps,

00:58:16.640 --> 00:58:19.700
but this is exactly-- because
the steps are exactly the same

00:58:19.700 --> 00:58:21.280
as the ones we did before.

00:58:21.280 --> 00:58:26.120
So we express b as in form--
in terms of the columns.

00:58:26.120 --> 00:58:28.140
And then that
product essentially

00:58:28.140 --> 00:58:31.400
becomes the linear combination
of the columns of b.

00:58:38.300 --> 00:58:41.240
Is it clear how we got to-- how
we define the Hamiltonian here?

00:58:44.440 --> 00:58:49.380
So now, we want to enforce
our optimality condition such

00:58:49.380 --> 00:58:55.540
that the u has to be a
minimizer of the Hamiltonian.

00:58:55.540 --> 00:58:58.540
And, again, in this case, the
only terms that depend on u

00:58:58.540 --> 00:59:02.860
are the first and the third.

00:59:02.860 --> 00:59:07.820
And so this boils down to
taking the minimum over u

00:59:07.820 --> 00:59:13.860
of the sum that goes
from 1 to m of ci times

00:59:13.860 --> 00:59:20.120
the absolute value of ui
plus p transpose bi, ui.

00:59:23.830 --> 00:59:26.700
Now, again, the u's are--

00:59:26.700 --> 00:59:29.240
the dimensions of
u are independent.

00:59:29.240 --> 00:59:31.070
And so we can do
exactly the same trick

00:59:31.070 --> 00:59:35.470
that minimizing the summation
is equal and equivalent

00:59:35.470 --> 00:59:37.670
to minimizing the
individual summation

00:59:37.670 --> 00:59:40.210
term for all i's from 1 to n.

00:59:45.070 --> 00:59:49.310
And so this means that it's
equal to minimizing over ui's

00:59:49.310 --> 00:59:57.670
of ci times the absolute value
of ui plus p transpose bi, ui.

00:59:57.670 --> 01:00:05.550
And this is for all
i's from 1 to m.

01:00:14.014 --> 01:00:23.350
So now, let's write this
term slightly more nicely.

01:00:23.350 --> 01:00:33.670
So this is equal to ci plus
p transpose bi times ui

01:00:33.670 --> 01:00:42.790
if ui is greater or
equal than 0 and is

01:00:42.790 --> 01:00:47.870
equal to negative ci
plus P transpose bi,

01:00:47.870 --> 01:00:53.610
ui if ui is less
than or equal than 0.

01:00:57.350 --> 01:01:00.930
And so we want to
minimize this object.

01:01:00.930 --> 01:01:06.150
And, again, we have to go
in a case-by-case analysis.

01:01:06.150 --> 01:01:22.350
So if p transpose bi is
greater than ci, what happens

01:01:22.350 --> 01:01:27.630
is that this term is positive.

01:01:27.630 --> 01:01:33.170
And so what u would I pick here
if I want to minimize this term?

01:01:44.296 --> 01:01:46.490
[INAUDIBLE]

01:01:46.490 --> 01:01:46.990
Sorry.

01:01:46.990 --> 01:01:48.690
I think I heard a 0.

01:01:48.690 --> 01:01:50.210
Yes, which is correct.

01:01:50.210 --> 01:01:52.410
Did I hear you mean
something else or--

01:01:52.410 --> 01:01:52.910
no.

01:01:52.910 --> 01:01:55.270
So indeed, it is 0.

01:01:55.270 --> 01:01:59.230
And so indeed-- so in the case
where p transpose bi is greater

01:01:59.230 --> 01:02:01.310
than or equal to c than--

01:02:01.310 --> 01:02:04.850
greater than ci, this
is greater than 0,

01:02:04.850 --> 01:02:09.470
which means that, in this case,
I would pick ui equals to 0.

01:02:09.470 --> 01:02:12.870
If we're still in that case,
then this other condition

01:02:12.870 --> 01:02:16.070
would also be positive.

01:02:16.070 --> 01:02:18.030
So this term here
is also positive.

01:02:18.030 --> 01:02:23.990
And so here, the ui that I
would pick would be Mi minus.

01:02:23.990 --> 01:02:27.750
And so in between these two
cases then for this case

01:02:27.750 --> 01:02:34.170
here, ui star would
be equal to MI minus.

01:02:37.436 --> 01:02:42.710
Now we do another case, that
is, the case where p transpose

01:02:42.710 --> 01:02:48.990
bi is between 0 and ci.

01:02:48.990 --> 01:02:52.830
If this is the case,
this is still positive.

01:02:52.830 --> 01:02:58.750
So here, I would also
pick ui equals 0.

01:02:58.750 --> 01:03:03.830
If b transpose bi
is between 0 and ci,

01:03:03.830 --> 01:03:07.070
this term here is negative.

01:03:07.070 --> 01:03:09.950
And so the ui that
I would have to pick

01:03:09.950 --> 01:03:14.230
would also be 0 to minimize.

01:03:14.230 --> 01:03:20.910
And so essentially, in this
case, u star i is equal to 0.

01:03:24.790 --> 01:03:28.430
Now, just by
mirroring, I-- yeah,

01:03:28.430 --> 01:03:31.950
we would go through the other
two remaining cases, which

01:03:31.950 --> 01:03:43.230
essentially are that p transpose
bi are between negative ci and 0

01:03:43.230 --> 01:03:51.550
and the fact that p transpose
bi is smaller than negative ci.

01:03:51.550 --> 01:03:55.110
So these would be
the other two cases.

01:03:55.110 --> 01:04:00.150
And here, we would get
that ui star is equal to 0

01:04:00.150 --> 01:04:03.510
and that ui star
is equal Mi plus.

01:04:03.510 --> 01:04:05.110
This is just by mirroring.

01:04:11.270 --> 01:04:13.430
And so, again, we
reached the point

01:04:13.430 --> 01:04:20.470
where we have expressed u from
the third of our boundary--

01:04:20.470 --> 01:04:24.600
from our necessary
optimality conditions.

01:04:24.600 --> 01:04:26.160
And we see that we are--

01:04:26.160 --> 01:04:30.300
again, we are assuming we're not
in the singular arc conditions.

01:04:30.300 --> 01:04:35.680
So we are ignoring the equal
cases of these examples here.

01:04:35.680 --> 01:04:38.920
But we have, again,
pinpointed the structure

01:04:38.920 --> 01:04:40.300
of our control profile.

01:04:40.300 --> 01:04:45.560
And this is the one that you
can see here on the slide

01:04:45.560 --> 01:04:50.600
where ui, depending on the
value of p transpose bi,

01:04:50.600 --> 01:04:54.320
is essentially either at
its minimum value or 0

01:04:54.320 --> 01:04:56.220
or at its maximum value.

01:04:56.220 --> 01:04:59.720
And this is known as
bang-off-bang control.

01:04:59.720 --> 01:05:03.280
And if you think about
what we're doing here,

01:05:03.280 --> 01:05:06.480
it is quite intuitive, again,
because this essentially

01:05:06.480 --> 01:05:08.960
describes control
profiles where I

01:05:08.960 --> 01:05:11.320
want to get as quickly
as possible into a state

01:05:11.320 --> 01:05:16.160
where I'm essentially
coasting through my dynamics,

01:05:16.160 --> 01:05:18.400
where I can do
nothing essentially,

01:05:18.400 --> 01:05:23.840
and then maybe only at the end
do another impulsive control.

01:05:23.840 --> 01:05:26.620
And this aligns with the
objective of minimizing fuel.

01:05:31.240 --> 01:05:33.480
Any questions on this example?

01:05:33.480 --> 01:05:38.560
I think here, it's easy to
get lost in the technicalities

01:05:38.560 --> 01:05:40.358
of solving this minimization.

01:05:40.358 --> 01:05:42.400
But I hope that at a high
level, what we're doing

01:05:42.400 --> 01:05:45.960
is starting to become clear,
because we're essentially

01:05:45.960 --> 01:05:49.440
repeating ourselves over and
over where we are enforcing

01:05:49.440 --> 01:05:51.240
our optimality conditions.

01:05:51.240 --> 01:05:54.040
By defining the Hamiltonian,
optimizing the Hamiltonian

01:05:54.040 --> 01:05:57.720
with respect to u, this
gives us a control profile,

01:05:57.720 --> 01:06:01.260
which in order to solve
the problem fully,

01:06:01.260 --> 01:06:03.880
we would have to then do
the appropriate substitution

01:06:03.880 --> 01:06:06.640
and solve the
differential equations.

01:06:06.640 --> 01:06:10.400
But this already gives us a
way to analyze the structure

01:06:10.400 --> 01:06:12.280
of our control profile.

01:06:12.280 --> 01:06:14.320
Yes.

01:06:14.320 --> 01:06:14.820
Yeah.

01:06:14.820 --> 01:06:15.320
Sorry.

01:06:15.320 --> 01:06:18.130
So when you were
doing the ci plus pt,

01:06:18.130 --> 01:06:20.768
bi that is just you
getting that derivative

01:06:20.768 --> 01:06:22.560
and seeing-- that just
gave me a derivative

01:06:22.560 --> 01:06:25.520
based on whatever the
absolute value is.

01:06:25.520 --> 01:06:28.680
And then you were looking at
the different options of pt,

01:06:28.680 --> 01:06:30.217
bi greater than ci.

01:06:30.217 --> 01:06:31.800
I'm a little bit
confused how then you

01:06:31.800 --> 01:06:34.000
got that they are
for the minimum based

01:06:34.000 --> 01:06:37.800
on pt, bi greater than ci,
because if pti is bigger,

01:06:37.800 --> 01:06:39.480
then that's not just
a positive values.

01:06:39.480 --> 01:06:42.000
And then the [INAUDIBLE]
of ui is just 0.

01:06:42.000 --> 01:06:46.198
And then so why is it now
that the minimum [INAUDIBLE]?

01:06:46.198 --> 01:06:48.240
You're talking about this
case here, these cases?

01:06:48.240 --> 01:06:49.680
Yeah.

01:06:49.680 --> 01:06:52.680
So here, remember that our
function we're optimizing

01:06:52.680 --> 01:06:54.180
is this one here.

01:06:54.180 --> 01:06:56.840
So it's a function where
we have an absolute value.

01:06:56.840 --> 01:06:57.440
Yes.

01:06:57.440 --> 01:07:01.180
And so we rewrote it
according to these two cases.

01:07:01.180 --> 01:07:01.880
Yes.

01:07:01.880 --> 01:07:06.000
Now, we are doing the
case where p transpose

01:07:06.000 --> 01:07:07.680
bi is greater than ci.

01:07:07.680 --> 01:07:12.600
And we want to find the minimum
across both of these values.

01:07:12.600 --> 01:07:18.280
So if p transpose bi is greater
