01:08:14.060 --> 01:08:17.420
but time was fixed.

01:08:17.420 --> 01:08:17.920
Yeah.

01:08:21.160 --> 01:08:24.479
So the third case of the
minimum energy problem--

01:08:24.479 --> 01:08:27.160
I would just go through
this quite quickly

01:08:27.160 --> 01:08:29.840
because it's exactly
the same case as the one

01:08:29.840 --> 01:08:34.560
that we did on our parabola
and the negative p2

01:08:34.560 --> 01:08:37.040
that we did just after
the definition of the PMP.

01:08:37.040 --> 01:08:39.479
So I would go through
this quite quickly.

01:08:39.479 --> 01:08:42.000
Again, here, the cases
that we're minimizing--

01:08:42.000 --> 01:08:44.660
R squared cost functional.

01:08:48.120 --> 01:08:50.800
And we would be doing the
exact same steps where

01:08:50.800 --> 01:08:52.760
we define the Hamiltonian.

01:08:52.760 --> 01:08:55.160
This Hamiltonian,
because of the-- we

01:08:55.160 --> 01:08:59.479
are assuming that R is
positive definite and diagonal.

01:08:59.479 --> 01:09:02.819
And so because of this,
everything nicely decouples.

01:09:02.819 --> 01:09:06.600
And so we get the same situation
where minimizing this summation

01:09:06.600 --> 01:09:10.040
terms is equal to minimizing
an individual term

01:09:10.040 --> 01:09:11.960
of the summation.

01:09:11.960 --> 01:09:14.200
And so since this
is quadratic, we

01:09:14.200 --> 01:09:16.960
set the derivative
of this equal to 0.

01:09:16.960 --> 01:09:21.399
And we will get this optimal
control, which is exactly

01:09:21.399 --> 01:09:23.960
the analog of the negative p2.

01:09:23.960 --> 01:09:26.680
So this is why I'm coasting
through this because this

01:09:26.680 --> 01:09:29.080
is exactly the same.

01:09:29.080 --> 01:09:31.939
And so we would get
our saturating control,

01:09:31.939 --> 01:09:34.319
which is the case we were
looking at the beginning.

01:09:34.319 --> 01:09:38.640
So we had our control saturating
when it's out of bounds, linear,

01:09:38.640 --> 01:09:40.800
and then saturating again.

01:09:40.800 --> 01:09:41.700
Yes.

01:09:41.700 --> 01:09:45.340
So we know to pick
R as a diagonal.

01:09:45.340 --> 01:09:48.040
If that makes the
calculation straightforward,

01:09:48.040 --> 01:09:49.160
why is that applicable?

01:09:49.160 --> 01:09:50.859
I mean, R-- if you
think about it,

01:09:50.859 --> 01:09:54.840
it's a term we pick because we
are defining the costs that we

01:09:54.840 --> 01:09:55.960
want to optimize.

01:09:55.960 --> 01:10:00.360
And so usually, it's good to
express diagonal cost matrices

01:10:00.360 --> 01:10:03.360
because it makes things easier.

01:10:03.360 --> 01:10:06.300
But we could also pick an
R that is not diagonal.

01:10:06.300 --> 01:10:06.800
Yeah.

01:10:12.900 --> 01:10:16.290
So I want to--

01:10:16.290 --> 01:10:18.930
so I have only 10 minutes
left, so I'll skip over this

01:10:18.930 --> 01:10:20.430
and finish it next time.

01:10:20.430 --> 01:10:23.010
But I want to give you a
hint on computational methods

01:10:23.010 --> 01:10:25.890
and show you how to solve
these problems in practice.

01:10:25.890 --> 01:10:28.730
And so basically,
until now, what we did

01:10:28.730 --> 01:10:31.570
is that we used our
optimality conditions

01:10:31.570 --> 01:10:36.250
to derive our systems of
differential equations

01:10:36.250 --> 01:10:40.730
and our minimization
over u condition.

01:10:40.730 --> 01:10:44.810
And then we always stopped
at the-- either we solve them

01:10:44.810 --> 01:10:46.870
analytically as we did
last week, or here,

01:10:46.870 --> 01:10:50.610
we stopped at expressing u
as a function of x and p's.

01:10:50.610 --> 01:10:54.250
And then we said that
here, we would substitute

01:10:54.250 --> 01:10:56.250
these back in our
differential equations,

01:10:56.250 --> 01:10:59.410
and we would solve them
through numerical solvers.

01:10:59.410 --> 01:11:03.090
And at a high level,
numerical techniques

01:11:03.090 --> 01:11:06.090
to solve two-point
boundary value problems

01:11:06.090 --> 01:11:10.330
divide themselves in between
indirect shooting methods

01:11:10.330 --> 01:11:12.690
and collocation methods.

01:11:12.690 --> 01:11:14.490
Now, we won't go
into the details

01:11:14.490 --> 01:11:17.530
of how these models,
these techniques

01:11:17.530 --> 01:11:21.370
solve two-point
boundary value problems.

01:11:21.370 --> 01:11:24.730
But at a high level, you
should see a shooting method

01:11:24.730 --> 01:11:27.650
as basically a method
that approximates

01:11:27.650 --> 01:11:30.810
a two-point boundary value
problem as an initial value

01:11:30.810 --> 01:11:35.330
problem where I take a guess
of what the initial value is.

01:11:35.330 --> 01:11:37.490
I propagate my system forward.

01:11:37.490 --> 01:11:40.870
I see my error with respect
to the final condition.

01:11:40.870 --> 01:11:42.330
I will get a residual.

01:11:42.330 --> 01:11:46.610
And then I update my
guesses iteratively.

01:11:46.610 --> 01:11:48.790
In direct collocation
methods, on the other hand,

01:11:48.790 --> 01:11:53.310
approximate the system dynamics
through basis functions,

01:11:53.310 --> 01:11:54.890
for example, polynomials.

01:11:54.890 --> 01:11:58.570
And they enforce the dynamics
and the boundary conditions

01:11:58.570 --> 01:12:02.290
at some specific points, which
are called collocation points.

01:12:02.290 --> 01:12:05.110
And so these are-- again,
don't read too much into this,

01:12:05.110 --> 01:12:06.450
but this is just--

01:12:06.450 --> 01:12:09.690
if you want to open the box
of how these numerical solvers

01:12:09.690 --> 01:12:11.170
typically operate,
they're either

01:12:11.170 --> 01:12:14.190
implementing a shooting method
or a collocation method.

01:12:17.290 --> 01:12:19.850
And we will be
looking at our example

01:12:19.850 --> 01:12:22.170
that it also came
up earlier today,

01:12:22.170 --> 01:12:23.850
where we are
essentially optimizing

01:12:23.850 --> 01:12:25.490
the particle on a line.

01:12:25.490 --> 01:12:28.450
And we solved this
analytically, where we basically

01:12:28.450 --> 01:12:32.290
derived the value of tf.

01:12:32.290 --> 01:12:36.130
But before getting there, I
wanted to just briefly introduce

01:12:36.130 --> 01:12:43.650
in an abstract example how these
numerical solvers look like.

01:12:43.650 --> 01:12:51.530
And specifically, we'll be using
the solve_bvp method in SciPy,

01:12:51.530 --> 01:12:55.570
where, at a high level,
the solve_bvp essentially

01:12:55.570 --> 01:13:00.370
takes in a fun argument, which
represents the right-hand side

01:13:00.370 --> 01:13:03.530
of our dynamics.

01:13:03.530 --> 01:13:06.250
BC, which represents
the boundary conditions,

01:13:06.250 --> 01:13:09.710
expressed as the residuals
of the boundary conditions.

01:13:09.710 --> 01:13:13.570
So it has to be
something equal to 0.

01:13:13.570 --> 01:13:17.290
t, which is like a mesh
of time, meaning we

01:13:17.290 --> 01:13:21.770
are discretizing time according
to some discrete bins.

01:13:21.770 --> 01:13:25.530
And so this will be just a
sequence of discretization time

01:13:25.530 --> 01:13:29.770
steps, which is just a
series of increasing numbers.

01:13:29.770 --> 01:13:34.110
z will be our
aggregated up variable.

01:13:34.110 --> 01:13:39.770
Essentially here, we will have
all our states and costates

01:13:39.770 --> 01:13:42.090
stacked together.

01:13:42.090 --> 01:13:44.450
And so to see this
in practice, let's

01:13:44.450 --> 01:13:51.050
look at a simple examples like
the one here on the slide.

01:13:51.050 --> 01:13:57.130
So, again, as I said, what
these solvers usually take

01:13:57.130 --> 01:14:00.490
is this solve_bvp
where we need to define

01:14:00.490 --> 01:14:04.350
first of all the function, so
our differential equations.

01:14:04.350 --> 01:14:10.770
And in the example
that we're looking at,

01:14:10.770 --> 01:14:14.730
we have this system
where we have

01:14:14.730 --> 01:14:19.170
z1 dot equals z2
and z2 dot equals

01:14:19.170 --> 01:14:24.050
negative absolute value of z1.

01:14:24.050 --> 01:14:26.770
And so here, we're
just basically stacking

01:14:26.770 --> 01:14:29.790
those dynamics.

01:14:29.790 --> 01:14:34.850
So we have-- remember that in
Python, counting starts from 0.

01:14:34.850 --> 01:14:38.670
So we basically have that
z1 dot is equal to z2.

01:14:38.670 --> 01:14:41.610
So here, we're just saying z2.

01:14:41.610 --> 01:14:46.490
And then we have z2 dot equals
negative absolute value of z1.

01:14:46.490 --> 01:14:51.490
And so we have negative
absolute value of z1.

01:14:51.490 --> 01:14:56.410
Now we need to enforce our
boundary conditions, which

01:14:56.410 --> 01:15:00.730
are that z1 at time 0 is
equal to 0 and z1 at time 4

01:15:00.730 --> 01:15:03.490
is equal to negative 2.

01:15:03.490 --> 01:15:09.130
And so what we have here-- so
za is intended for the boundary

01:15:09.130 --> 01:15:11.810
conditions at the initial time.

01:15:11.810 --> 01:15:16.810
And so we have za0 equal
to 0, which is essentially

01:15:16.810 --> 01:15:18.730
this term over here.

01:15:18.730 --> 01:15:25.770
And then we have
z1 4 plus 2 equals

01:15:25.770 --> 01:15:30.930
0, because we have to express
them in the residual form, which

01:15:30.930 --> 01:15:32.030
is what we have here.

01:15:34.570 --> 01:15:38.370
Now, we construct the t_mesh,
which essentially is, again,

01:15:38.370 --> 01:15:42.850
just a number which informs what
is our discretization of time,

01:15:42.850 --> 01:15:44.950
in this case, from 0 to 4.

01:15:44.950 --> 01:15:49.330
And we assume we have
five bins between 0 and 4.

01:15:49.330 --> 01:15:52.490
And z_guess is just
an initial guess

01:15:52.490 --> 01:15:57.810
of our variables at those times.

01:15:57.810 --> 01:16:00.170
So what happens if
we just run this

01:16:00.170 --> 01:16:03.970
is that basically, we get that
the differential equations are

01:16:03.970 --> 01:16:04.750
solved.

01:16:04.750 --> 01:16:09.020
So this is the plot of
z1 and z2 over time.

01:16:09.020 --> 01:16:11.620
So first of all, it
goes from 0 to 4.

01:16:11.620 --> 01:16:17.100
And note that z1 starts at
0, which was our boundary

01:16:17.100 --> 01:16:18.080
condition here.

01:16:18.080 --> 01:16:24.260
And it ends at negative 2, which
was our final time boundary

01:16:24.260 --> 01:16:26.380
condition.

01:16:26.380 --> 01:16:28.620
And then note that--

01:16:28.620 --> 01:16:35.760
so z2 is defined as the
time derivative of z1.

01:16:35.760 --> 01:16:41.980
And so when z2 is
positive, z1 grows.

01:16:41.980 --> 01:16:46.020
When z2 is negative,
z1 also decreases.

01:16:46.020 --> 01:16:47.780
So this is just a
visual inspection

01:16:47.780 --> 01:16:50.520
that we did indeed solve
these differential equations.

01:16:56.300 --> 01:16:58.720
Yeah, I think I'll take
some questions now,

01:16:58.720 --> 01:17:00.440
and then I'll wrap
this up next time.

01:17:00.440 --> 01:17:01.320
Yes.

01:17:01.320 --> 01:17:09.380
Is z2 completely
[INAUDIBLE] because you

01:17:09.380 --> 01:17:16.380
take the z2 dot equals negative
z1 and the [INAUDIBLE].

01:17:21.420 --> 01:17:24.780
So do you [INAUDIBLE]?

01:17:24.780 --> 01:17:27.820
In this case, z2
has to basically--

01:17:27.820 --> 01:17:29.700
is something that
always decreases,

01:17:29.700 --> 01:17:32.140
because the time
derivative of z2

01:17:32.140 --> 01:17:34.420
is negative and absolute value.

01:17:34.420 --> 01:17:36.900
And so if you also
inspect this visually,

01:17:36.900 --> 01:17:38.000
this is what is happening.

01:17:38.000 --> 01:17:40.860
But if you're talking about
other specific constraints

01:17:40.860 --> 01:17:43.680
on z2, in this case,
we don't have any.

01:17:43.680 --> 01:17:45.750
So it can start and
end at any values?

01:17:45.750 --> 01:17:46.250
Mm-hmm.

01:17:48.980 --> 01:17:50.100
All right.

01:17:50.100 --> 01:17:52.460
So see you on
Wednesday while we'll

01:17:52.460 --> 01:17:55.000
be wrapping this
computational methods.

01:17:55.000 --> 01:17:57.460
And then we'll introduce
